          {
            title: 'Tres y Dos',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Tres y Dos (Three and Two) is a simple Rummy-style game from the Dominican Republic, Cuba, and Puerto Rico. Players draw and discard to form a 'Full House' â€” three cards of one rank plus two cards of another rank. First to complete this hand wins.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Tres y Dos is a folk rummy-style game played across the Dominican Republic, Cuba, and Puerto Rico. Contributors report that most Dominicans who have ever played cards know the game; it is often learned in childhood. In Cuba, children sometimes bet marbles on each hand while adults may play for money. The game has no formal documented history; it is transmitted informally as a household and social pastime.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two or more players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Standard 52-card deck. Suits don't matter.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deal 5 cards each. Stock + Discard pile.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
